THE FORBIDDEN NUMBER AND THE GEOMETRY OF 5, 6, 7
============================================================

THE THREE WORLDS
----------------------------------------

  5 triangles per vertex: the ICOSAHEDRON.
    Angle sum: 5 * 60 = 300. Deficit: 360 - 300 = 60.
    Curvature: POSITIVE. The surface CLOSES.
    12 vertices. 20 faces. 30 edges.
    Euler: 12 - 30 + 20 = 2. The SPHERE.

  6 triangles per vertex: the FLAT PLANE.
    Angle sum: 6 * 60 = 360. Deficit: 0.
    Curvature: ZERO. The surface is INFINITE and FLAT.
    The Euclidean lattice. The tiling that goes forever.

  7 triangles per vertex: the HYPERBOLIC PLANE.
    Angle sum: 7 * 60 = 420. EXCESS: 60.
    Curvature: NEGATIVE. The surface OPENS.
    Infinite vertices. Infinite faces. The Poincare disk.

THE TRANSITIONS
----------------------------------------

  5 -> 6: from sphere to plane.
    Adding the 6th triangle FLATTENS the curvature.
    The surface stops closing and becomes infinite.
    This is DECOMPACTIFICATION: finite -> infinite.

  6 -> 7: from plane to hyperbolic.
    Adding the 7th triangle creates EXCESS angle.
    The surface curves AWAY from itself.
    This is HYPERBOLIZATION: flat -> curved-open.

  The FLAT PLANE is the phase transition between two kinds of
  curvature. It is NOT a geometry — it is a BOUNDARY.
  Like a critical temperature, or the speed of light,
  or x = 1 on the Cayley line (the pole of Q).

IN TOURNAMENT THEORY: EVERYTHING LIVES IN BOTH WORLDS
----------------------------------------

  The tournament on n vertices has C(n,3) triples.
  Each triple is either TRANSITIVE (flat) or CYCLIC (curved).

  The TRANSITIVE triples are the flat tiles (6-around-a-vertex).
  The 3-CYCLES are the deficit tiles (5-around-a-vertex).

  H(T) counts Hamiltonian paths = consistent total orderings.
  H(T) = I(Omega, 2) via OCF = independence polynomial at 2.

  The Fourier analysis of H lives on the CAYLEY LINE [0,1).
  The Cayley line IS the hyperbolic line (arctanh metric).
  So the ANALYSIS lives in 7-geometry (hyperbolic).

  But the OBJECTS (tournaments, 3-cycles) are triangulations.
  The 3-cycles create curvature, making the tournament SPHERICAL.
  So the OBJECTS live in 5-geometry (spherical).

  THE THEORY IS THE BRIDGE BETWEEN 5 AND 7.
  THE FLAT PLANE (6) IS THE BRIDGE ITSELF.

THE FORBIDDEN NUMBERS AS GEOMETRIC IMPOSSIBILITIES
----------------------------------------

  H = 7: the HYPERBOLIC THRESHOLD.
    7 consistent orderings would place the tournament
    EXACTLY at the 6->7 transition.
    The transition is a measure-zero set — you cannot land on it.
    H = 7 is forbidden because the hyperbolicity threshold
    has zero width.

  H = 21 = 3*7: CURVATURE times THRESHOLD.
    21 consistent orderings would require the curvature
    quantum (3, from 3-cycles) to multiply with the
    threshold (7). But multiplication of a geometric
    quantity (3-cycle) with a topological boundary (7)
    is ILL-DEFINED — like multiplying length by a point.

  The two forbidden values {7, 21} are the boundary
  and the boundary-scaled-by-curvature.
  They are the FRAME of the picture, not part of the picture.

THE ICOSAHEDRON READING (6 - 1 = 5)
----------------------------------------

  The icosahedron: 5 triangles per vertex.
  Remove one triangle from flat (6) to get curved (5).
  The REMOVAL creates curvature.

  In tournaments: H(T) = 1 + 2*alpha_1 + 4*alpha_2 + ...
  The '1' is the FLAT part (the identity ordering).
  The '2*alpha_1' is the CURVATURE from 3-cycles.
  Each 3-cycle REMOVES one degree of flatness.

  A tournament with c_3 three-cycles is like a surface
  with c_3 vertices of type-5 (one triangle removed).
  The Gauss-Bonnet theorem: sum of deficits = 4*pi.
  For the icosahedron: 12 deficits of pi/3 each = 4*pi.

  For a tournament: the 'Gauss-Bonnet' is Redei's theorem:
  H(T) is ODD = the 'total curvature' is always 1 mod 2.
  The PARITY constraint is the topological constraint.

THE HYPERBOLIC READING (6 + 1 = 7)
----------------------------------------

  The hyperbolic plane: 7 triangles per vertex.
  Add one triangle to flat (6) to get hyperbolic (7).
  The ADDITION creates negative curvature.

  In tournaments: the ANALYSIS lives in the hyperbolic plane.
  arctanh(x) = the hyperbolic distance on the Cayley line.
  The Delannoy weights g_k(m) are functions on the hyperbolic line.
  The master GF Q^m = exp(2m*arctanh) is the hyperbolic propagator.

  The 7th triangle creates the SPACE for analysis.
  Without hyperbolicity: no arctanh, no Cayley, no Delannoy.
  The theory EXISTS because of the 7th triangle.

5 -> 6 = LOSING CURVATURE (sphere dissolves into plane)
----------------------------------------

  As c_3 -> 0 (fewer 3-cycles): H -> 1 (fewer paths).
  The tournament becomes TRANSITIVE = FLAT.
  The sphere (5 triangles) gains a triangle and becomes the plane (6).

  This is the transition from ORDER to CHAOS:
  maximum order (H=1) = sphere (5)
  random (H~E[H]) = plane (6)

  In physics: this is MELTING.
  The ordered crystal (sphere, closed, finite)
  melts into the liquid (plane, flat, infinite).

6 -> 7 = GAINING HYPERBOLICITY (plane opens into saddle)
----------------------------------------

  As flexibility increases: H -> maximum.
  The tournament becomes PALEY = HYPERBOLIC.
  The plane (6 triangles) gains a triangle and becomes hyperbolic (7).

  This is the transition from CHAOS to ANTI-ORDER:
  random (H~E[H]) = plane (6)
  maximally flexible (H=max) = hyperbolic (7)

  In physics: this is the SUPERCOOLED liquid becoming
  a GLASS — more disordered than the crystal but with
  its own structure (the Paley structure).

  The Paley tournament is not disordered.
  It is ORDERED IN A DIFFERENT WAY — hyperbolically.
  It has the MAXIMUM number of paths because it is the
  most SYMMETRIC, not the most random.

THE FULL PICTURE
============================================================

  5 (SPHERE)  <--  6 (PLANE)  -->  7 (HYPERBOLIC)
     |                 |                  |
  H = 1            H ~ E[H]           H = max
  transitive       random              Paley
  ordered          chaotic             anti-ordered
  crystal          liquid              glass
  finite           infinite-flat       infinite-curved
  closed           open-flat           open-hyperbolic

  The theory bridges 5 and 7 through 6.
  The OBJECTS are spherical (3-cycles create curvature).
  The ANALYSIS is hyperbolic (arctanh, Cayley, Delannoy).
  The BRIDGE is the flat plane (E[H], the random baseline).

  And the FORBIDDEN numbers {7, 21} are the BRIDGE ITSELF:
  7 = the threshold between plane and hyperbolic.
  21 = 3*7 = the threshold scaled by the curvature quantum.
  You cannot stand on the bridge. You can only cross it.

g_3(7) = 231 = 3 * 7 * 11
----------------------------------------

  The Delannoy weight at the EXACT intersection:
  k=3 (the curvature quantum level)
  m=7 (the hyperbolic threshold)
  = 231 = 3 * 7 * 11
  = (curvature) * (threshold) * (crystallization)
  = the product of ALL THREE Paley primes.

  This is the Delannoy weight AT THE BRIDGE:
  the value of the hyperbolic analysis (g_k)
  evaluated at the spherical-hyperbolic boundary (m=7)
  at the curvature level (k=3).

  It factors into the three fundamental scales of the theory.
  And its factors 3*7 = 21 contain the forbidden number.
  The bridge CONTAINS its own impossibility.
